<edge source="mid_east_energy_shock::crude_oil_supply_availab" target="ai_safety_militarization::regulatory_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">A sudden drop in crude supply reframes energy shortfalls as national-security emergencies, prompting governments to fast-track rulemaking, export controls and oversight of dual‑use technologies (including AI) to protect critical infrastructure and manage risks.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">mid_east_energy_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_safety_militarization</data>
</edge>
<edge source="mid_east_energy_shock::crude_oil_supply_availab" target="global_rate_repricing::policy_interest_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A sudden drop in crude supply from the Middle East raises headline inflation and inflation expectations through higher transport and energy costs, prompting central banks to raise policy rates to defend inflation targets.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">mid_east_energy_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">global_rate_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="mid_east_energy_shock::crude_oil_supply_availab" target="global_rate_repricing::nominal_yield_curve" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Reduced crude availability increases expected future inflation and term premia, pushing up nominal yields across maturities and steepening/raising the yield curve as investors demand compensation for higher expected inflation and risk.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">mid_east_energy_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">global_rate_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="mid_east_energy_shock::crude_oil_supply_availab" target="russia_ukraine_war::sanction_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">A substantive drop in available crude from the Middle East increases buyers' dependence on alternative exporters (notably Russia) and strengthens those suppliers' diplomatic leverage, prompting importing governments to dial back the severity or scope of sanctions to secure uninterrupted supplies.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">mid_east_energy_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
