  <data key="d36">A rise in policy interest rates raises domestic borrowing and refinancing costs for upstream producers and service contractors, delaying maintenance and new field investment and thereby reducing near‑term crude output availability.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_rate_repricing</data>
  <data key="d39">mid_east_energy_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_rate_repricing::policy_interest_rate" target="russia_ukraine_war::defense_aid_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising policy rates increase governments' borrowing costs and debt‑service burdens, tightening near‑term fiscal space and political willingness to approve new or sustained outlays for foreign military assistance.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_rate_repricing</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
